{
  "image_name": "3edc677ea5e6740481946c64db325df9.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a signaling model for how a regulatory T cell interacts with a lymphatic endothelial cell (LEC) to promote immune suppression and tissue entry. At the cell interface, the Treg cell recognizes MHC class II through the TCR, and PD1 on the Treg binds PDL1 on the endothelial cell. These contacts trigger pathways in both cells.\n\nIn the Treg, signaling branches toward phosphorylated NF-κB/p65 and the PI3K–AKT–mTOR axis. These pathways are linked to production of suppressive mediators such as IL-10 and TGFβ, increased proliferation, and expression of inhibitory receptors including PD1, GITR, and CTLA4, culminating in overall immune suppression.\n\nIn the endothelial cell, the same interaction activates ERK1/2, NF-κB/p65, and PI3K–AKT. ERK1/2 and NF-κB appear to increase VCAM1, which then supports transendothelial migration (TEM). The PI3K–AKT branch is shown leading to VE-cadherin degradation and increased endothelial permeability.\n\nOverall, the diagram suggests that PD1–PDL1 and antigen presentation cooperate to reinforce Treg suppressive function while simultaneously making the lymphatic endothelium more permissive for Treg passage.",
